WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:03.700
Lithium is the lightest metal.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:08.840
We all know that sodium is pretty light as a metal.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:13.620
If you drop it onto water and forget the
reaction, it floats.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.960
You can see the orange sodium flame.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:17.960
Oh, It's on the camera.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:24.660
Of course, lithium will do the same, though, there are differences which I will show you in a minute.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:32.860
But what I wanted to see was whether we could demonstrate how much lighter lithium was than sodium.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:39.360
We've never tried this experiment before, but I asked Neil, our technician, to drop

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:45.080
a piece of sodium and a piece of lithium of approximately the same size, at the same time

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:54.660
into beakers of hexane. Hexane is similar to gasoline, or petrol, and when he dropped them, it was really striking

00:00:54.660 --> 00:01:04.700
The sodium sank like a stone, because gasoline is much less dense than water. But lithium bobbed around

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:07.780
like a little boat. If you look carefully, they both started bubbling,

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:16.000
because we didn't use dry hexane, and both samples of hexane contained just a little bit of water, that

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:23.380
started reacting. And of course, that prompted Neil to want to drop the lumps of metal into real water.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.860
So we tried the experiment again, dropping lithium and sodium into water.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:38.100
Of course, this time, both of them floated.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:44.320
But lithium is less reactive towards water than sodium.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:49.640
It's a smaller atom, the electrons are more tightly held, and the reaction is slower.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:56.520
With the sodium, it was really quite surprising, it went off with a real bang,

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:03.820
and the beaker ended up in the back of the fume cupboard.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:12.820
And the sodium was gone. And the lithium quietly buzzed around on the surface of the water.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:20.860
And when Neil encouraged it with a flame, we got a beautiful red colour, the characteristic flame colour, of lithium.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:28.980
The other experiment we tried was burning lithium in air, and comparing it with burning lithium in nitrogen.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:38.520
N2  has the strongest bond between any two atoms of the same sort in the periodic table, so you need

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:45.420
a lot of energy to break them apart. Because lithium is small, it can form very tight bonds with nitrogen,

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:55.560
and so I read frequently that lithium will burn in nitrogen. So I persuaded Neil to set fire to some lithium

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.620
and it burned really brightly in air,

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:06.260
and then poured liquid nitrogen into a beaker, to fill it up with nitrogen gas.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:17.020
Nitrogen boils at -200C or -196C, so the gas was pretty cold in the beaker. And then he plunged the burning

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:22.560
lithium into the beaker. Now here, there was a difference of opinion between me and

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.580
Neil, I thought the reaction was a great success,

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:28.580
Neil was a bit disappointed,

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:31.960
because the lithium just glowed red.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:35.660
But what I saw, was that this is not a very big piece of metal

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.260
being plunged into really cold gas

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:43.060
and it kept on glowing red. On and on and on,

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:48.860
whereas if it had just been put out
because it wasn't reacting with nitrogen

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:53.660
it would have lost its color very
quickly. So I felt that it was a

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:55.340
reasonable demonstration.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:59.660
I've tried to do the same demonstration with magnesium which is also meant to

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:00.920
burn in nitrogen,

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:06.260
I demonstrated it for 25 years, to successive generations of students and

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:12.050
didn't work once. I knew it wouldn't work but every time the students thought:

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:14.900
"Stupid professor! Doesn't work!"

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:18.920
Lithium is used for treating bipolar
disorder,

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:23.660
sometimes called manic depression. This is a very distressing disease which is

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:30.140
caused by imbalance of chemicals in the
brain which causes people sometimes to

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:34.730
be really manic and other times so
depressed that they can't get out of bed.

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:43.310
The application which you use probably most often for lithium is in factories.

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:50.030
This is not a new idea I found an old
scientific american from 1967.

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:57.940
And inside there is an advertisement here, with the picture of Edison's desk. On this desk

00:04:57.949 --> 00:05:01.720
when he died was a large can of lithium.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:08.220
This is an advertisement from a
company that sells lithium or sold lithium, in those days.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:12.020
The reason that Edison had
lithium on his desk was that he

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:16.200
realized it could potentially make
really good batteries.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:20.760
[Brady] Is that the known reason, or is that just what Foote mineral company is suggesting?

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.020
I think that's what they're suggesting.

00:05:23.029 --> 00:05:26.779
And since he didn't make lithium
batteries, perhaps we'll never know.

00:05:26.779 --> 00:05:32.089
So there's one final and the important
application of lithium which is in

00:05:32.089 --> 00:05:34.459
making hydrogen bombs.

00:05:34.459 --> 00:05:40.459
Let me just remind you that an atom bomb is formed by atoms

00:05:40.459 --> 00:05:45.539
usually of uranium that could also
be plutonium, splitting apart.

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:51.210
This a called fission bomb, a hydrogen bomb is inherently different.

00:05:51.889 --> 00:05:57.139
The Atomic Energy Commission and the Department of
Defense have conducted important

00:05:57.139 --> 00:06:02.819
tests of an experimental device based on a thermonuclear principle.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:09.420
Bits come together. So two atoms, for example, two deuterium atoms.

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:11.370
But what is deuterium?

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:15.120
I was coming to that. An atom of this
kind of hydrogen has the simplest

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:21.120
nucleus known. But hydrogen exists in two other isotopic forms: deuterium, which

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:26.780
has a neutron in addition to the proton, and tritium, which has two neutrons and one proton.

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:30.380
Atoms of hydrogen with mass
two, rather than mass one,

00:06:30.389 --> 00:06:37.290
come together to form an atom of
helium. The first hydrogen bombs that

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:42.160
were made, and they really ought to be called deuterium bombs not hydrogen bombs,

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:49.760
involved liquid deuterium. And so
the bomb really looked like a small

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:54.730
chemical factory because they were big
tanks of liquid deuterium and nobody

00:06:54.880 --> 00:07:00.000
could imagine carrying this on an
airplane for actually bombing.

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:03.600
This is the first full-scale test of a hydrogen device.

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:08.470
If the reaction goes, we are in the
thermonuclear era.

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:14.060
five, four, three, two, one..

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:23.979
The breakthrough came when it was
realized that you could use the compound of

00:07:23.979 --> 00:07:30.179
lithium and deuterium this is lithium deuteride, LiD.

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:32.919
Which is chemically the same as LiH,

00:07:32.919 --> 00:07:37.659
but with a heavier isotope. What happens with that sort of bomb is that you

00:07:37.659 --> 00:07:43.509
trigger it with an atom bomb that
produces a lot of neutrons and the

00:07:43.509 --> 00:07:50.259
neutrons turn some of the lithium atoms into tritium, hydrogen of mass three, and

00:07:50.259 --> 00:07:54.939
then the deuterium and the tritium fuse together giving out energy.

00:07:54.939 --> 00:07:59.740
Lithium deuteride is a solid which is
stable at room temperature. In principle

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:07.300
you could make lithium tritide, LiT,
but tritium is highly radioactive, and decays

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:10.059
away with a half-life of 12 years.

00:08:10.059 --> 00:08:15.430
So you have to keep on replacing your
bombs. So you could build the bomb

00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:20.229
without the huge containers of liquid
and you can make something was still

00:08:20.229 --> 00:08:24.939
pretty big, but in principle could go in
a giant bomber.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:39.940
The first test of a lithium deuteride
bomb was carried out, I think in 1954.

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:46.200
We feel of the report of this important test should give the failures as well as the successes.

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:48.820
The test name was called Castle Bravo.

00:08:48.829 --> 00:08:51.649
The date was march, 1, 1954

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:55.900
This photograph was taken from an
airplane at 50 miles.

00:08:57.589 --> 00:09:03.569
The width of the fireball at this time,
about three seconds after detonation, was four miles.

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:07.060
And it was really too successful.

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:11.630
The power of the bomb was three times bigger than had been calculated.

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:17.280
The tremendous field resulted in a serious fallout situation at Bikini and certain

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:20.360
other atolls downwind from ground zero.

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:30.560
The residents of the islands nearby had not been evacuated far enough and several people were exposed to radiation

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:36.769
in the most unfortunate manner. But the principal was demonstrated. Of course the

00:09:36.769 --> 00:09:39.589
whole technology is horrific.

00:09:39.589 --> 00:09:44.810
But it's interesting to see how lithium
played a part. But to end on a much

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:46.430
happier note.

00:09:46.430 --> 00:09:54.860
Lithium is a light metal. Hydrogen can be used to fuel cars, using fuel cells that

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:57.350
converts hydrogen to electricity.

00:09:57.350 --> 00:10:03.170
So now there is a lot of research to see whether lithium hydride or a derivative

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:10.160
of lithium hydride can be used to store
hydrogen in the tank of your car.

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:17.200
Which Sarin is one of them unfortunately terrorists can get hold of it I've got a

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:18.880
molecule of Sarin here

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:23.950
Not real Sarin. Not real Sarin, no, that would be illegal to make that, I've

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:27.160
made it out of straws and these coloured balls there.

